<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Irish Parliamentary Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1882">1882</a>–<a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Irish Parliamentary Party was the Irish nationalist party in the UK House of Commons that campaigned for Home Rule. Organised under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-stewart-parnell">Charles Stewart Parnell</a>, it held the balance of power in 1885–1886 and again from 1910 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-redmond">John Redmond</a>, but after the Easter Rising and the conscription crisis it was swept aside by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinn-fein">Sinn Féin</a> in the general election of 1918.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party grew out of the Home Rule League, which Isaac Butt founded after leaving the Irish Conservative Party in 1873. After the 1874 general election forty-six Home Rule members met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> and formed a separate group in the Commons, but Butt could win no major party to bills for Ireland. Younger members led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-stewart-parnell">Charles Stewart Parnell</a>, Joseph Biggar and John Dillon turned in 1876–1877 to parliamentary obstructionism, proposing amendments to almost every bill, which revived the group and brought Parnell to the fore.</p><p>Parnell became president of Michael Davitt's Irish National Land League in 1879. The Kilmainham Treaty of April 1882, a truce with the government in which he undertook to move against agrarian crime, marked a turning point in his leadership, though it lost him the support of John Devoy's Irish-American Fenians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Parnell's party was tightly disciplined; one author described it as a regiment led by Parnell and Michael Davitt.The party pledge of 1884 bound every member to sit, act and vote with it, under a whip, Richard Power. Members were paid expense allowances from party funds long before other MPs received state pay in 1911, which let middle-class men such as William O'Brien and D. D. Sheehan sit in parliament. By the early 1890s more than half of the Irish members came from the professions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ewart-gladstone">William Ewart Gladstone</a>'s first Home Rule Bill was defeated in 1886 by 341 votes to 311. In 1890 Parnell was named co-respondent in Katharine O'Shea's divorce, and the party split; Gladstone's second bill passed the Commons in 1893 but was rejected by 419 peers to 41. The factions reunited in 1900 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-redmond">John Redmond</a>, and the party pressed for land purchase, local government and labourers' housing acts.</p><p>After the Parliament Act 1911 curbed the Lords, a Home Rule bill introduced in 1912 was passed but suspended when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> began. Redmond's call for the Irish Volunteers to support the war split them, over 142,000 forming the National Volunteers. In the 1918 election the party won 220,837 votes but only 6 seats, losing 78 of its 84, and Dillon lost his own. Its remnants formed the Nationalist Party in Northern Ireland under Joe Devlin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Irish Parliamentary Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Home Rule, demanded by the <b>Irish Parliamentary Party</b> under Charles Stewart Parnell and later John Redmond, was opposed by Unionists, concentrated in Ulster, who signed the Ulster Covenant in 1912 and formed the Ulster Volunteers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
